# TODO - RF/PHY/regulatory

## Required reference workflow and files

Use `.references/` at the repository root for all retained source research.
Populate it with `python3 -B tools/references/download.py`; use `--offline`
to verify existing sources. Search ignored references explicitly with
`rg --hidden --no-ignore -g '!**/.git/**' -n 'symbol|register|error' .references/`
and enumerate files with `rg --files --hidden --no-ignore .references/`.
Whenever a behavior, API or missing implementation is unclear, search these
sources and the web for the evidence needed (prefer upstream source, published
specifications and official documentation). Record exact revisions, source
hashes, adaptation scope, destinations and licenses in the single
`.references/ledger.jsonl`. Preserve upstream notices; downloaded code is not
proof of implementation or runtime success.

Concrete files for this task (paths are relative to the repository root):

- `.references/linux/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath9k/hw.c` — reset and PLL sequencing.
- `.references/linux/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath9k/ar9003_hw.c` — revision-specific initialization.
- `.references/linux/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath9k/ar9003_phy.c` — synthesizer/channel and PHY controls.
- `.references/linux/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath9k/ar9003_calib.c` — calibration sequencing.
- `.references/linux/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath9k/ar9565_1p0_initvals.h` — AR9565 register tables and gains.
- `.references/linux/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath9k/ar9565_1p1_initvals.h` — AR9565 1.1 radio postamble.
- `.references/linux/net/wireless/reg.c` — regulatory policy; adapt only supported target scope.

## Objective

Finish revision-aware radio power/reset, PLL/synthesizer, gains, channel
selection, ANI/noise handling, RF-kill, calibration and legal per-channel TX
power constraints. Source must reject unknown board or country states. **No
live signal or transmission tests.**

## Acceptance

- [ ] Implement/review the stated source and integrate on main.
- [ ] Block radio TX without valid regulatory country and board power data.
- [ ] Ground decisions in public code, published standards and SDK/KDK references.
- [ ] Record unresolved assumptions and fail-closed behavior.
- [ ] Complete with source/static evidence, NEVER requiring a physical Dell test.

## Required source deliverables

- [ ] Execute revision-aware wake/reset, PLL/synthesizer and ordered PHY/RF
      initialization through the actual PCI owner, with required delays,
      bounded waits, error propagation and recovery state invalidation.
- [ ] Preserve HT20/HT40, every required RX/TX gain choice and the AR9565 1.1
      radio postamble. Apply actual board calibration, antenna/diversity,
      RF-kill and Bluetooth-coexistence programming, not recipe flags alone.
- [ ] Implement channel changes, ANI/noise handling and required calibration
      scheduling, including suspend/resume reinitialization and RF-kill races.
- [ ] Intersect board limits with a legitimate runtime country/channel policy;
      implement CTL/target interpolation, antenna/EIRP constraints and actual
      calibrated per-rate TPC/register/descriptor conversion, including HT40
      and channel-14 restrictions. Candidate numbers alone cannot enable TX.
- [ ] Enforce the common TX gate before management, data and automatic MAC
      responses: valid calibration, permitted channel/power and RF-kill state.

## Start here and next implementation step

The native owner now executes Ar9565RtcPower through actual PCI MMIO.
It also derives the regulator and crystal capacitor settings from a live
same-epoch physical EEPROM/OTP lease, scrubs physical keycache first, samples
EEPROM-selected RF-kill GPIO as a real input, and guards each PMU/XTAL MMIO
with a fresh switch read when the pin is present. A configured blocked or
unstable pin aborts startup; a missing pin stays **unknown**, not authorized.
See commits `8cc4e8f`, `7991ff4` and `13b2a9f`.
Native source commit `ccae41c` now also checks the epoch-current physical
RF-kill GPIO at runtime: an immutable read-only observer double-samples with a
100-us settle and rejects blocked, unstable, missing, stale-epoch, short-BAR or
unreadable states. Native TX, RX dispatch, key/port admission and Tahoe owner
snapshot consume this fail-closed result. The EDMA admission callback also
rechecks the same GPIO before ring start or descriptor submission, and its
fault path revokes channel/TPC evidence and wipes temporary packet buffers.
These are source-level safety gates, not PHY setup, RF-switch interrupts or a
physical verification of blocked radio emissions: automatic MAC responses,
quiescence on asynchronous RF-kill, channel/INI/calibration, TPC and genuine
native receiver authorization still require full native integration.
Ar9565InitCatalog selects canonical INI tables and Ar9565RfIni executes them;
the obsolete permanently false whole-driver recipe flag has been replaced
with the precise tablesComplete stage. Explicit ReceiveOnly authorization
allows the real table stage without fabricating TX power/country approval;
it does not enable TX, automatic ACK/CTS or probe requests. Required board,
antenna, permitted receive channel, RF-kill and coexistence proofs remain.
Ar9565Power returns numerical candidates, not calibrated register values or
transmission authority. PLL, synthesizer and RF-bus executors are implemented
in separate source modules. The ath9k OFDM/short-GI timing stage is also
implemented with canonical HT20/HT40 center math and per-register readback
(commit `810d661`). Regulator, board modal, coexistence and OFDM timing are
now listed in the normal Xcode target, but those latter three stages are
**not yet executed** by the native controller.
Commit `fcc10ee` makes the portable radio sequence consume the same immutable
EEPROM snapshot for its board-modal stage as for
preflight, even if earlier MMIO callbacks modify caller-owned EEPROM bytes.
Synthetic C++14/C++17 sanitizer fixtures, direct strict Clang-Tidy and
formatting passed. This is a source-only TOCTOU correction, **not** evidence
that the native PCI owner executes the full RF sequence.
The receive-only RF source orchestrator now caches AR9565 ANI first-step and
spur-immunity register defaults from its applied, epoch-current PHY after
receiver calibration. `Ar9565Ani` also provides bounded ath9k-relative
first-step/spur controls with per-MMIO owner rechecks, readback and reset on
an ambiguous partial write (offline synthetic C++14/C++17 sanitizer fixtures).
The Xcode production target includes this module, but **the PCI owner does
not yet execute the RF sequence**, and no runtime PHY-error sampling/ANI
scheduler, channel lease or complete spur policy exists. The source stage
cannot grant RX/TX, country, antenna, coexistence or operational readiness.

Source commit `f7e04a7` adds the separate, chain-zero 2.4 GHz open-loop
transmit calibration register stage in the **existing normal Xcode target**.
It adapts ath9k `ar9003_hw_power_control_override` to program the AR9565
OLPC gain-delta field, error-estimator mode, thermal-slope and factory
calibration temperature reference. The stage uses a restored EEPROM-derived
`PowerCandidates` snapshot, enforces explicit TX authorization and an
owner-epoch check on every MMIO read/write, masks unrelated bits, verifies
posted writes and retains a reset obligation after any partial write or
readback uncertainty. Synthetic C++14/C++17 ASan+UBSan, direct strict
Clang-Tidy for source and test and Clang-Format checks passed. **Native PCI
code does not invoke this stage** and it is not sufficient for live
voltage/temperature tracking, true calibrated per-rate descriptor power,
country/antenna/BT permission, physical RF transmission or Wi-Fi readiness.
The RF/PHY/regulatory TODO stays open.

Actual ordered RF INI, PLL, synthesizer, BT-safe antenna allocation,
calibration and country/channel
policy remain blocking; source-only tests do not prove physical reception.

Implement the actual ordered radio executor after A2/A3 contracts exist:
reset/wake -> PLL -> revision/mode/gain INI phases -> board values -> calibration
-> channel/power programming -> RX/automatic-response policy. Handle every
failure by invalidating affected channel/key/queue/calibration state, with
quiescence coordinated through the owner. Preserve per-revision choices.

Start from ath9k [hw.c](https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath9k/hw.c)
(ath9k_hw_init_pll/reset), [ar9003_hw.c](https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath9k/ar9003_hw.c)
(mode/gain recipes), [ar9003_calib.c](https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath9k/ar9003_calib.c)
(PCI calibration path), and [ar9003_mci.c](https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath9k/ar9003_mci.c)
(reset/coexistence sequencing). Scope each port to AR9565, not similarly named
AR9561/AR956x SoCs. Use ar9003_eeprom.c calibration/power consumers to connect
real board fields; do not stop at raw summaries.

Country information from an AP is an input to policy, not sole authority to
relax EEPROM/board restrictions. Implement the intersection with a trusted
platform country/channel policy; an unknown origin cannot silently grant TX.
See Linux Wireless [regulatory processing](https://wireless.docs.kernel.org/en/latest/en/developers/regulatory/processing_rules.html).
This is a source contract, not a claim that Linux regulatory APIs exist on macOS.

Useful fake-clock/register tests: short reset pulse, stalled/backwards clock,
revision-specific postamble, malformed HT40 geometry, stale calibration epoch,
and denial of management/data/automatic TX before board and country approval.

